==Translingual==

===Etymology===
? may derive from Qo, with the Q written over the o, an abbreviation of Latin {{m|la|quaestio||question}}, placed at the end of a question to mark it as such.<ref>Alexander Humez, 1987, ''A B C et cetera: the life & times of the Roman alphabet''</ref>

===Punctuation mark===
{{mul-punctuation mark}}

# Marks a preceding passage written in [[Appendix:Latin script|Latin script]] as a [[question]].
# {{lb|mul|comics}} {{n-g|Used [[by oneself|by itself]] to convey that a [[character]] is [[confused]].}}
#: Character #1: "I have no time to explain! Have you seen a Big Bad Wolf blowing down various houses?"
#: Character #2: "'''?'''"
# For the reversed question mark used in some right-to-left languages (including Arabic and Persian), see {{m|mul|؟|sc=Zsym}}.

===Symbol===
{{mul-symbol}}

# Indicates an unknown [[word]], [[phrase]], [[text]] or an unknown numerical [[value]].
#*{{quote-book
|year=2009
|author=Terry Stickels
|title=Math Puzzles and Brainteasers, Grades 3-5: Over 300 Puzzles that Teach Math and Problem-Solving Skills
|pageurl=http://books.google.com.br/books?id=ceZaQbH2y4AC&pg=PA6#v=onepage&q&f=false
|isbn=9780470564677
|page=6
|publisher=John Wiley & Sons}}
#*:What is the next number in the sequence below?
#*:1 &nbsp;&nbsp; 4 &nbsp;&nbsp; 9 &nbsp;&nbsp; 16 &nbsp;&nbsp; 25 &nbsp;&nbsp; 36 &nbsp;&nbsp; <u>&nbsp;'''?'''&nbsp;</u>
# {{lb|mul|chess}} In [[algebraic notation]], marks a [[bad]] move.
# {{lb|mul|programming}} A [[wildcard]] for one [[character]] in [[query language]].
# {{lb|mul|programming}} The [[ternary operator]] in some programming languages.
# {{lb|mul|regex}} Detects [[zero]] or [[one]] occurences of the preceding element.
#: ''The string <code>colou?r</code> matches both "color" and "colour".''
# {{lb|mul|networking}} In a [[URL]], begins a [[query string]] (a series of [[data]] formatted as field-value pairs).
#: ''[http://example.com/form.asp?name1=value1&name2=value2 http://example.com/form.asp'''?'''name1=value1&name2=value2]''

====Usage notes====
* Its English name is variously [[question mark]], [[interrogation mark]], [[interrogation point]], [[eroteme]].

